{
 "id": "2020-ye-tunnel",
 "name": "Tunnel Portal Aesthetics: Nine Orthogonal Design Schemes",
 "description": "Method replication with the paper's own renderings: participants approach nine 3D-modelled design schemes for the same motorway headwall tunnel portal (Banlun Tunnel, Funing-Longliu Expressway, Yunnan) and grade each on the paper's five-level comment set (great, good, ordinary, relatively bad, bad) by first impression. The schemes are the paper's L9(3^4) orthogonal design crossing portal line (straight, step type, arch), colour (blue, green, concrete), greening (delicate, simple, none) and texture (mural, simple treatment, none). As in the paper, everyone grades all nine twice. Rebuilt from Ye, Su, Wei, Xu & Liang (2020), PLOS ONE 15(9): e0238762, with the renderings printed in its Fig 10 (CC BY 4.0). Analysis as in the paper: normalise the grade counts per scheme and multiply by the evaluation set {95, 85, 75, 65, 60} to get each scheme's aesthetic score, then range analysis over the orthogonal table to rank the four factors and pick the best level of each. Differences from the original study: (1) the original used a driving simulator (Euro Truck Simulator 2, up to 80 km/h); here each approach is a 7 s zoom into the published rendering, with no real motion, steering or traffic; (2) the renderings are cropped from the published figure (about 640 x 320 px); (3) no 10 min rest after each portal; (4) the paper does not report the presentation order; here each round uses a new random order, and the nine schemes are shown side by side before round 2; (5) the original sample was 10 male graduate students with over three years of driving experience; the age, gender and driving-experience questions are our wording.",
