  "description": "Method replication with the paper's own renderings: participants approach nine 3D-modelled design schemes for the same motorway headwall tunnel portal (Banlun Tunnel) and grade the aesthetics of each on the paper's five-level comment set (great, good, ordinary, relatively bad, bad), quickly and by first impression. The nine schemes are the paper's L9(3^4) orthogonal design crossing portal line (straight, step type, arch), colour (blue, green, concrete), greening (delicate, simple, none) and texture (mural, simple treatment, none). As in the paper, everyone grades all nine twice: a first round, then, once familiar with all nine, a second round. Rebuilt from Ye, Su, Wei, Xu & Liang (2020), PLOS ONE 15(9): e0238762, with the renderings printed in its Fig 10 (CC BY 4.0). Analysis as in the paper: count the grades per scheme, normalise to a membership vector and multiply by the evaluation set {95, 85, 75, 65, 60} to get each scheme's aesthetic score; then range analysis over the orthogonal table (mean score at each level of each factor; the factor with the largest range matters most) to pick the best level of each factor. Differences from the original study: (1) the original used a driving simulator (Euro Truck Simulator 2, up to 80 km/h) with participants driving through each portal; here each approach is a 7 s zoom into the published rendering, so there is no real motion, steering or traffic; (2) the renderings are cropped from the published figure (about 640 x 320 px); (3) the original drivers rested about 10 min after each portal; here there is no rest; (4) the paper does not report the presentation order; here each round uses a new random order, and before round 2 the nine schemes are shown side by side so that participants are familiar with all of them; (5) the original sample was 10 male graduate students with more than three years of driving experience; the age, gender and driving-experience questions here are our wording.",
